00:46:38.141 --> 00:46:40.970
this case they're working
with [inaudible] and so on.

00:46:40.971 --> 00:46:44.145
We got to the theory
that was most practical,

00:46:44.146 --> 00:46:46.405
if it kind of pushed on this.

00:46:46.406 --> 00:46:47.960
&gt;&gt; It's fantastic to hear.

00:46:47.961 --> 00:46:50.200
&gt;&gt; I would like to
give you an example of

00:46:50.201 --> 00:46:52.590
a good case of long-term
thinking, which actually,

00:46:52.591 --> 00:46:54.350
was at the Institute
for Advanced Study,

00:46:54.351 --> 00:46:56.330
the mathematician
Vladimir Voevodsky,

00:46:56.331 --> 00:46:58.090
who unfortunately
passed away last year.

00:46:58.091 --> 00:47:01.345
But, he was a great
algebraic geometer,

00:47:01.346 --> 00:47:02.870
got the Fields medal,

00:47:02.871 --> 00:47:05.140
then found a mistake
in one of his papers.

00:47:05.141 --> 00:47:07.020
He was very surprised
to find a mistake,

00:47:07.021 --> 00:47:09.780
but even more surprised
that nobody cared.

00:47:10.750 --> 00:47:13.080
Then, he thought, well, I have to

00:47:13.081 --> 00:47:15.405
rewrite the foundations
of mathematics,

00:47:15.406 --> 00:47:18.930
so that the computers
can actually understand,

00:47:18.931 --> 00:47:22.225
in his case, abstract
homotopy theory.

00:47:22.226 --> 00:47:24.525
So, he then spent 10 years,

00:47:24.526 --> 00:47:26.360
essentially without
producing any paper.

00:47:26.361 --> 00:47:30.730
I would say it's like a
whale diving very deep,

00:47:30.731 --> 00:47:31.990
and then at some point,
you're worried, well,

00:47:31.991 --> 00:47:33.650
will he come back to the surface.

00:47:33.651 --> 00:47:37.375
He did, and I think it was
quite the successful program,

00:47:37.376 --> 00:47:39.040
and it might have a big impact,

00:47:39.041 --> 00:47:40.620
but it makes me think,

00:47:40.621 --> 00:47:42.780
do we have places
where somebody can

00:47:42.781 --> 00:47:44.590
be underwater for 10 years?

00:47:44.591 --> 00:47:45.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's something.

00:47:45.431 --> 00:47:47.660
&gt;&gt; In the end, we all benefit.

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.650
When he was not
producing anything,

00:47:50.651 --> 00:47:53.610
are you worried that he
would have come out I

00:47:53.611 --> 00:47:56.945
think well if somebody spends
10 years thinking about it,

00:47:56.946 --> 00:47:58.630
and you find a common result,

00:47:58.631 --> 00:48:00.040
you have a 10-year advantage,

00:48:00.041 --> 00:48:02.545
which is terrific too.

00:48:02.546 --> 00:48:04.410
&gt;&gt; It's an amazing example,

00:48:04.411 --> 00:48:08.370
and there are other
amazing examples that are

00:48:08.371 --> 00:48:11.430
also less amazing and
less famous examples

00:48:11.431 --> 00:48:13.450
to spend 10 years.

00:48:13.451 --> 00:48:19.620
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:48:19.621 --> 00:48:22.425
&gt;&gt; Eva, can you save
that story for later?

00:48:22.426 --> 00:48:25.930
&gt;&gt; I really like that our field,

00:48:25.931 --> 00:48:28.475
and actually your field also,

00:48:28.476 --> 00:48:31.980
economics and theoretical
computer science.

00:48:32.470 --> 00:48:34.775
When you write a paper,

00:48:34.776 --> 00:48:36.680
you have to start by explaining

00:48:36.681 --> 00:48:39.290
