00:48:44.846 --> 00:48:46.730
of the field of

00:48:46.731 --> 00:48:48.240
the universe because there are

00:48:48.241 --> 00:48:50.230
very few results of that sort.

00:48:50.231 --> 00:48:53.240
So, most days, day
in and day out,

00:48:53.241 --> 00:48:55.130
each of us cannot or I

00:48:55.131 --> 00:48:57.625
cannot produce
results of that sort.

00:48:57.626 --> 00:49:00.675
But, I have to write
an introduction that explains,

00:49:00.676 --> 00:49:03.505
why do you need to care about,

00:49:03.506 --> 00:49:05.310
why is this the step in

00:49:05.311 --> 00:49:06.850
a possible direction
where there is

00:49:06.851 --> 00:49:08.860
a big hill that we
really care about?

00:49:08.861 --> 00:49:11.400
I admit the machine learning
people now have it easy.

00:49:11.401 --> 00:49:13.700
There is a very big clear
big hill over there.

00:49:13.701 --> 00:49:15.650
But, everything we're working on,

00:49:15.651 --> 00:49:19.030
we are constantly pushed
to explain the goal.

00:49:19.031 --> 00:49:21.195
That's usually
interests of all of

00:49:21.196 --> 00:49:23.965
our papers, and these ideas,

00:49:23.966 --> 00:49:25.555
people sometimes do that,

00:49:25.556 --> 00:49:27.370
and occasionally
they're successful,

00:49:27.371 --> 00:49:29.985
but I think sometimes
they're less.

00:49:29.986 --> 00:49:31.610
I really like that I'm

00:49:31.611 --> 00:49:33.715
getting pushed and
I guess there is

00:49:33.716 --> 00:49:36.100
one example from my early years

00:49:36.101 --> 00:49:37.640
that I've been
pretty public about,

00:49:37.641 --> 00:49:39.990
where I wrote a paper where in

00:49:39.991 --> 00:49:43.870
the break after I gave the
talk László Lovász very,

00:49:43.871 --> 00:49:46.750
very gently, and very
politely came over that,

00:49:46.751 --> 00:49:49.610
"Yes, but why do you care?"

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:54.580
I was so embarrassed that
I can't defend myself,

00:49:54.581 --> 00:49:56.360
that it took me, I forgot,

00:49:56.361 --> 00:49:58.945
many years till I even
wrote down the result.

00:49:58.946 --> 00:50:00.110
I did write it down,

00:50:00.111 --> 00:50:04.660
it is actually published in
Journal of JCT, Journal.

00:50:04.661 --> 00:50:06.370
&gt;&gt; Combinatorial Theory.

00:50:06.371 --> 00:50:07.450
&gt;&gt; Combinatorial Theory.
Thank you. So, it

00:50:07.451 --> 00:50:09.205
was a hard paper,

00:50:09.206 --> 00:50:11.830
but I think I learned a lesson,

00:50:11.831 --> 00:50:13.600
and this is the lesson I promote,

00:50:13.601 --> 00:50:17.040
is that we need to think
even before we get involved.

00:50:17.041 --> 00:50:18.420
Why do we care?

00:50:18.421 --> 00:50:21.380
I guess he knew why he
cared because he was

00:50:21.381 --> 00:50:24.730
so famous and well established.

00:50:24.731 --> 00:50:26.690
The community trusted him.

00:50:26.691 --> 00:50:29.810
But, I think the fact that
we regularly have to write

00:50:29.811 --> 00:50:31.480
about our partial results and

00:50:31.481 --> 00:50:33.645
explain that this is a good core,

00:50:33.646 --> 00:50:35.650
has two giant benefits; one,

00:50:35.651 --> 00:50:38.125
it makes us think about
this which is good;

00:50:38.126 --> 00:50:40.430
and second, we
recruit our friends,

00:50:40.431 --> 00:50:42.090
or our colleagues to help.

00:50:42.091 --> 00:50:44.840
These much of the results
that we're achieving,

00:50:44.841 --> 00:50:46.915
they're not a single
person's achievement.

00:50:46.916 --> 00:50:49.840
It's anything that the

00:50:49.841 --> 00:50:51.690
two of them talked
about for crypto,

00:50:51.691 --> 00:50:53.740
from error-correcting code,

00:50:53.741 --> 00:50:55.440
there's a community
working on this.

00:50:55.441 --> 00:50:56.875
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you want
to add something.

00:50:56.876 --> 00:51:01.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I think
the long-term is that,

00:51:01.031 --> 00:51:03.990
I agree with you completely
that you need to have

00:51:03.991 --> 00:51:06.960
a goal in mind that you
think is worth going for.

00:51:06.961 --> 00:51:08.460
But, now, the goal can

00:51:08.461 --> 00:51:10.380
be one that you think
that will take you

00:51:10.381 --> 00:51:12.390
a long time to achieve or one

00:51:12.391 --> 00:51:15.450
that you just want to pay for
because we need your penny.

00:51:20.300 --> 00:51:22.915
Clearly we need both
because like you said,

00:51:22.916 --> 00:51:24.925
life is happening now
and you want to advance.

00:51:24.926 --> 00:51:26.650
But, also clearly we

00:51:26.651 --> 00:51:28.825
want the long-term one
because that's where the joy,

00:51:28.826 --> 00:51:31.015
the really big community
ideas come from.

00:51:31.016 --> 00:51:32.830
Some of us, I'm not saying all,

00:51:32.831 --> 00:51:35.830
every one of those scholarship
should spend now,

00:51:35.831 --> 00:51:39.420
go 10 years underwater,
and think deeply.

00:51:39.421 --> 00:51:41.960
But, some people
should because we need

00:51:41.961 --> 00:51:44.495
some good ideas and it's
dangerous, I agree with you.

00:51:44.496 --> 00:51:48.380
You can sink in and die there.

00:51:48.381 --> 00:51:51.100
So, it is a dangerous journey.

00:51:51.101 --> 00:51:53.970
But I think that's where
it's an important one,

00:51:53.971 --> 00:51:56.325
and that's where a lot of
wonderful things happen.

00:51:56.326 --> 00:51:58.440
&gt;&gt; Actually, one comment on that,

00:51:58.441 --> 00:52:00.960
I think look,
stepping back a bit,

00:52:00.961 --> 00:52:04.260
when we think about
who supports research?

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:08.045
Why they support it and
what their goals are?

00:52:08.046 --> 00:52:09.895
That's a very difficult part of

00:52:09.896 --> 00:52:12.240
research because
there's a huge value in

00:52:12.241 --> 00:52:14.440
these deep dives and
new discoveries that are

00:52:14.441 --> 00:52:16.945
going to be transformative
many years later.

00:52:16.946 --> 00:52:18.950
Often those aren't the ones

00:52:18.951 --> 00:52:21.245
that are going to have
immediate payoffs.

00:52:21.246 --> 00:52:24.330
You need large
benevolent organizations

00:52:24.331 --> 00:52:25.889
like Microsoft, or universities,

00:52:25.890 --> 00:52:27.800
or something that are
willing to fund labs,

00:52:27.801 --> 00:52:33.145
that allow people to take
deep dives and that's not-

00:52:33.146 --> 00:52:35.725
&gt;&gt; I noticed that people who do

00:52:35.726 --> 00:52:38.440
a completely different
financial investment.

00:52:38.441 --> 00:52:40.465
Often, they have a risk project.

00:52:40.466 --> 00:52:41.790
They have to really spend

00:52:41.791 --> 00:52:44.500
so many percentage of
risk and they can do it.

00:52:44.501 --> 00:52:45.640
They can distribute it in their-.

00:52:45.641 --> 00:52:54.130
[inaudible].

00:52:54.131 --> 00:52:55.360
They have a risk budget and

00:52:55.361 --> 00:52:57.700
they really measure so to say,

00:52:57.701 --> 00:53:01.605
the level of risky and
that risky investment.

00:53:01.606 --> 00:53:03.555
I don't think
actually we do this,

00:53:03.556 --> 00:53:07.515
at least not in a very
conscious supported way.

00:53:07.516 --> 00:53:08.190
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:53:08.191 --> 00:53:08.775
&gt;&gt; Should we do it or?

00:53:08.776 --> 00:53:10.995
&gt;&gt;Yeah. I think that
somehow it's difficult that

00:53:10.996 --> 00:53:14.100
research is done in
a very self-organized system.

00:53:14.101 --> 00:53:16.845
It's people out there just
doing what they want.

00:53:16.846 --> 00:53:19.370
There are some incentives,

00:53:19.371 --> 00:53:21.810
and [inaudible] and tenure,

00:53:21.811 --> 00:53:23.900
and you have to keep
producing at some level.

00:53:23.901 --> 00:53:26.285
You have to justify
what you're doing.

00:53:26.286 --> 00:53:29.280
There's landmarks and goals,

00:53:29.281 --> 00:53:32.450
but the overall picture

00:53:32.451 --> 00:53:34.485
of how we're invested
and who's doing what,

00:53:34.486 --> 00:53:36.210
there's very little coordination.

00:53:36.211 --> 00:53:39.910
It's just like a lot of
humans like ants marching

00:53:39.911 --> 00:53:41.620
around all doing their thing and

00:53:41.621 --> 00:53:44.215
hoping that we coordinate
and produce good things.

00:53:44.216 --> 00:53:46.505
There's enough of us that
good things happen but.

00:53:46.506 --> 00:53:48.135
&gt;&gt; Does any of you believe

00:53:48.136 --> 00:53:50.010
that it's an optimal
distribution?

00:53:50.011 --> 00:53:53.440
&gt;&gt; I'm very wary of any form
of metrics being used to

00:53:53.441 --> 00:53:54.990
determine what's a good research

00:53:54.991 --> 00:53:57.070
and who is a good researcher.

00:53:57.071 --> 00:54:00.840
One of the things that you
have to keep in mind is

00:54:00.841 --> 00:54:04.525
that we are taking a wide
spectrum of people over here,

00:54:04.526 --> 00:54:06.260
and then going to the
very, very, very,

00:54:06.261 --> 00:54:07.945
tail ends to determine,

00:54:07.946 --> 00:54:11.215
"These are the people who
want to be researchers."

00:54:11.216 --> 00:54:14.380
Applying the theory of

00:54:14.381 --> 00:54:16.400
predictability which is
supposed to be somewhere

00:54:16.401 --> 00:54:19.465
sitting around the middle
of the bell curve to

00:54:19.466 --> 00:54:20.960
a population which is sitting at

00:54:20.961 --> 00:54:24.375
the extreme seems to be
completely dangerous to me.

00:54:24.376 --> 00:54:26.330
Unfortunately, this is getting

00:54:26.331 --> 00:54:28.730
practiced too commonly
in universities,

00:54:28.731 --> 00:54:31.760
tenure systems,
promotion cases, etc.

00:54:31.761 --> 00:54:34.115
We really have to
be wary about it.

00:54:34.116 --> 00:54:36.270
Everyone of us knows by

00:54:36.271 --> 00:54:40.210
personal familiarity
that the people

00:54:40.211 --> 00:54:42.545
that they admire were
not the ones who,

00:54:42.546 --> 00:54:44.365
sort of, is the metrics.

00:54:44.366 --> 00:54:45.600
These are the ones
that came up with

00:54:45.601 --> 00:54:47.120
the brilliant ideas and probably

00:54:47.121 --> 00:54:50.475
came up with the brilliant ideas
almost out of nowhere.

00:54:50.476 --> 00:54:52.655
At the same time to see,

00:54:52.656 --> 00:54:54.490
"Should we now try to

00:54:54.491 --> 00:54:56.720
manage this process,"
is very dangerous.

00:54:56.721 --> 00:54:58.105
I love by the way

00:54:58.106 --> 00:55:01.255
the Microsoft motto or

00:55:01.256 --> 00:55:03.650
principles by which
they were hiring,

00:55:03.651 --> 00:55:06.990
and they were managing
the research labs which was,

00:55:06.991 --> 00:55:08.480
"We decide who we hire,

00:55:08.481 --> 00:55:10.285
we don't decide what they do."

00:55:10.286 --> 00:55:12.510
I think it was
a wonderful principle and

00:55:12.511 --> 00:55:14.650
I hope they're going
to continue this.

00:55:14.651 --> 00:55:19.770
It was when I was here and
after that if you like.

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:22.770
&gt;&gt; I think it's fair to say

00:55:22.771 --> 00:55:26.240
that both that principle
and the long-term goal,

00:55:26.241 --> 00:55:27.330
and it's great to be here with

00:55:27.331 --> 00:55:29.465
an organization that values this.

00:55:29.466 --> 00:55:32.200
I hear a lot of complaints
that in some sense

00:55:32.201 --> 00:55:35.460
that open space is shrinking.

00:55:35.461 --> 00:55:37.610
That for instance, 50 years ago,

00:55:37.611 --> 00:55:40.580
even big famously at
Bell Labs and a lot

00:55:40.581 --> 00:55:43.470
of places that were
producing many,

00:55:43.471 --> 00:55:46.090
many Nobel Prizes,
and somehow had

00:55:46.091 --> 00:55:48.260
more oxygen or something

00:55:48.261 --> 00:55:50.620
than typically now
you see around.

00:55:50.621 --> 00:55:51.810
Any of you agree,

00:55:51.811 --> 00:55:53.560
is the world getting better,

00:55:53.561 --> 00:55:56.875
it's getting different,
what's happening?

00:55:56.876 --> 00:55:58.960
&gt;&gt;I think that at
the end of the day,

00:55:58.961 --> 00:56:00.190
you're asking
whether there should

00:56:00.191 --> 00:56:02.105
be a managing research?

00:56:02.106 --> 00:56:04.340
I completely agree with
Michael 100 percent.

00:56:04.341 --> 00:56:07.370
There is a difference by
definition because you

00:56:07.371 --> 00:56:08.570
need money for it
and someone needs to

00:56:08.571 --> 00:56:11.100
decide how to
distribute the money.

00:56:11.101 --> 00:56:15.100
That new distribution is

00:56:15.101 --> 00:56:19.430
a definition of what the world
think of us, important.

00:56:19.431 --> 00:56:21.455
I mean importance is
where the money goes,

00:56:21.456 --> 00:56:25.320
so that's [inaudible] . There

00:56:25.321 --> 00:56:28.435
is such a thing happening
by necessity in a sense.

00:56:28.436 --> 00:56:32.019
&gt;&gt; I think humans are good
at reacting to crisis.

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:35.220
They're not good at
long-term investments,

00:56:35.221 --> 00:56:37.955
and that's a really
difficult thing to do.

00:56:37.956 --> 00:56:41.110
To say, "Look, we
want to fund this.

00:56:41.111 --> 00:56:43.150
It's going to be great.
Let all these people take

00:56:43.151 --> 00:56:46.355
deep dives and we'll see
30 years from now what happens."

00:56:46.356 --> 00:56:48.320
Instead you're looking at,

00:56:48.321 --> 00:56:51.725
"We've got this pressing problem
and let's solve it."

00:56:51.726 --> 00:56:54.520
It's a very difficult thing
to do and the funding becomes

00:56:54.521 --> 00:56:56.060
essential because these things

00:56:56.061 --> 00:56:57.905
can't be done without
that funding.

00:56:57.906 --> 00:56:59.220
&gt;&gt; I want to object,

00:56:59.221 --> 00:57:01.085
there are two issues
we are raising,

00:57:01.086 --> 00:57:04.770
the deep dive as in
going into an area which

00:57:04.771 --> 00:57:08.420
doesn't produces
interesting and amazing things

00:57:08.421 --> 00:57:10.775
and not necessarily
have immediate impact,

00:57:10.776 --> 00:57:13.225
and that you have to
do it all by yourself.

00:57:13.226 --> 00:57:15.545
I don't think you have to
do it all by yourself.

00:57:15.546 --> 00:57:18.040
I think the pressure

00:57:18.041 --> 00:57:22.520
to publish or pressure to talk
about pressure resilience,

00:57:22.521 --> 00:57:25.570
and sometimes some people
take it the wrong

00:57:25.571 --> 00:57:27.400
way and work on something that

00:57:27.401 --> 00:57:30.030
doesn't have a long-term
goal and I object.

00:57:30.031 --> 00:57:31.970
The positive impact of

00:57:31.971 --> 00:57:33.635
the very same thing is

00:57:33.636 --> 00:57:35.555
that when you publish
your pressure is that

00:57:35.556 --> 00:57:37.165
two words every goal then

00:57:37.166 --> 00:57:39.400
usually there's a whole community

00:57:39.401 --> 00:57:41.340
out there who suddenly sees that,

00:57:41.341 --> 00:57:45.135
"Good point, the goal
might be reachable.

00:57:45.136 --> 00:57:48.945
Let me welcome this."
There's a whole part there.

00:57:48.946 --> 00:57:50.850
Your pressure is that,

00:57:50.851 --> 00:57:54.585
they're recruiting a community
which is not against

00:57:54.586 --> 00:57:59.790
the main goal, idea.

00:57:59.791 --> 00:58:02.880
&gt;&gt; I think we are
getting close to the end

00:58:02.881 --> 00:58:05.310
of the first- Perhaps
one more question.

00:58:05.311 --> 00:58:08.130
Yes, please. Then we'll finish.

00:58:08.340 --> 00:58:11.530
&gt;&gt; Do you think the lag will

00:58:11.531 --> 00:58:14.040
change now that people
are communicating better,

00:58:14.041 --> 00:58:16.400
there's no necessary
a 30 year lag between

00:58:16.401 --> 00:58:17.690
the time that
somebody has a great

00:58:17.691 --> 00:58:19.990
theoretical result
and it's applied?

00:58:19.991 --> 00:58:22.810
&gt;&gt; Perhaps that's a good
question and then I'll finish

00:58:22.811 --> 00:58:26.035
by each ask you
a very short question about,

00:58:26.036 --> 00:58:29.190
what's the future of thinking,

00:58:29.191 --> 00:58:30.950
of how do you see.

00:58:30.951 --> 00:58:33.655
Perhaps mention one element

00:58:33.656 --> 00:58:35.680
that might change
the nature of theory,

00:58:35.681 --> 00:58:37.335
the nature of long-term thinking

00:58:37.336 --> 00:58:38.920
when we are looking
in the future.

00:58:38.921 --> 00:58:40.980
If you're here in
10 years celebrating

00:58:40.981 --> 00:58:43.580
10 years [inaudible]
what will be different?

00:58:43.581 --> 00:58:46.650
&gt;&gt; Let me make
a quick answer to Adam,

00:58:46.651 --> 00:58:47.840
and this actually Kevin

00:58:47.841 --> 00:58:49.030
Leyton-Brown is in
the audience and

00:58:49.031 --> 00:58:52.160
we've done two MOOCs together.

00:58:52.161 --> 00:58:53.770
I think now the dissemination of

00:58:53.771 --> 00:58:56.065
knowledge is really amazing.

00:58:56.066 --> 00:58:57.740
Being able to teach
game theory to

00:58:57.741 --> 00:58:59.655
people around the world in

00:58:59.656 --> 00:59:02.995
a large scale that they would
never have had access to,

00:59:02.996 --> 00:59:05.990
means that there's
a very different landscape.

00:59:05.991 --> 00:59:07.510
That we don't quite
understand yet,

00:59:07.511 --> 00:59:11.350
but it's very very encouraging
when people who otherwise

00:59:11.351 --> 00:59:12.650
would have no access to

00:59:12.651 --> 00:59:15.270
this knowledge can now
become part of the team,

00:59:15.271 --> 00:59:17.140
become recruited, and help

00:59:17.141 --> 00:59:18.620
producing future knowledge that's

00:59:18.621 --> 00:59:19.970
going to advance the society.

00:59:19.971 --> 00:59:22.310
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:59:22.311 --> 00:59:26.590
&gt;&gt; Also a quick reaction
to Adam's question,

00:59:26.591 --> 00:59:29.780
I think as we're
communicating faster,

00:59:29.781 --> 00:59:32.025
I'd say not necessarily better,

00:59:32.026 --> 00:59:34.430
one of the advantages is

00:59:34.431 --> 00:59:36.750
of course you hear
all the good news very quickly,

00:59:36.751 --> 00:59:39.155
also hear all the fake
news very quickly.

00:59:39.156 --> 00:59:41.980
This does not apply
only to realms

00:59:41.981 --> 00:59:44.410
like politics and society,

00:59:44.411 --> 00:59:47.385
it's also applying to
scientific results.

00:59:47.386 --> 00:59:48.950
We all heard about the proof of

00:59:48.951 --> 00:59:51.265
the Riemann hypothesis
a few weeks back,

00:59:51.266 --> 00:59:54.335
and these kinds of events.

00:59:54.336 --> 00:59:57.050
We do have to worry a lot about,

00:59:57.051 --> 00:59:59.810
there is a lot of
material being generated.

00:59:59.811 --> 01:00:01.790
Simply the volume is very high.

01:00:01.791 --> 01:00:04.355
Even if you have
infinite bandwidth coming in,

01:00:04.356 --> 01:00:07.625
there is a limited bandwidth
going into my brain.

01:00:07.626 --> 01:00:10.715
I think we're going to have,

01:00:10.716 --> 01:00:13.290
this will be
a process which will,

01:00:13.291 --> 01:00:16.950
somethings become better,
maybe your geographical

01:00:16.951 --> 01:00:18.705
distance from the center of

01:00:18.706 --> 01:00:21.865
the discovery is no
longer as relevant.

01:00:21.866 --> 01:00:24.810
But having intellectual mass

01:00:24.811 --> 01:00:27.450
at a place where you are is
still going to be relevant.

01:00:27.451 --> 01:00:30.180
You are alone in
this isolated place,

01:00:30.181 --> 01:00:32.680
you can be very
well-connected on the web to

01:00:32.681 --> 01:00:36.235
every piece of development
and still not really get it.

01:00:36.236 --> 01:00:38.815
&gt;&gt; Eva. One short.

01:00:38.816 --> 01:00:41.440
&gt;&gt; I think I love Adam's comment
and I think that's

01:00:41.441 --> 01:00:44.650
[inaudible] of the future
to connect the

01:00:44.651 --> 01:00:46.870
Machine learning is
an excellent barrier

01:00:46.871 --> 01:00:49.710
where this is really
happening more powerfully.

01:00:49.711 --> 01:00:51.380
By the time people submit

01:00:51.381 --> 01:00:54.060
their paper and machine
learning to NIPS or [inaudible]

01:00:54.061 --> 01:00:55.630
usually tests on
these same number of

01:00:55.631 --> 01:00:58.575
citations because they
put it on archive first.

01:00:58.576 --> 01:01:00.355
People are already
starting to work

01:01:00.356 --> 01:01:02.060
on follow-up and using it.

01:01:02.061 --> 01:01:04.230
It is amazing and I think,

01:01:04.231 --> 01:01:06.220
I don't know if we'd
be ever as fast

01:01:06.221 --> 01:01:09.000
as the machine
learning community is,

01:01:09.001 --> 01:01:11.820
but this speed of

01:01:11.821 --> 01:01:14.890
people reacting to
each other's idea improving.

01:01:14.891 --> 01:01:17.660
I think we'll help science
go further faster.

01:01:17.661 --> 01:01:18.360
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:01:18.361 --> 01:01:19.900
&gt;&gt; No, it's okay [inaudible].

01:01:19.901 --> 01:01:21.350
&gt;&gt; Okay, terrific.

01:01:21.351 --> 01:01:23.600
Well I want to thank
all of you for

01:01:23.601 --> 01:01:28.825
being awake and participating.

01:01:28.826 --> 01:01:33.069
I think in the self-congratulatory
nature of academics,

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:35.755
we want to celebrate

01:01:35.756 --> 01:01:37.565
this wonderful institution here

01:01:37.566 --> 01:01:40.090
and happy 10th anniversary.

01:01:40.091 --> 01:01:42.710
&gt;&gt;Thank you. Let's
thank the panel.

